(5420868, 776006, 93565, '<td valign="top">Hello, Im from the Critique Club; just stopping by drop you a comment!<br/>As a relatively new member, I hope youre enjoying DPC, and Im glad to see youve already entered a couple challenges.<br/>At first glance, this image wouldnt strike me as a language entry, for a couple reasons. After reading your comments, I understand where you were going with this and I like the idea of depicting commerce as a language, truly the only real international language, in fact. Unfortunately, viewers cannot see your comments during voting, so the fact that this is a fruit vendor would have been lost on me, as I didnt realize that without the help of your details. I also dont immediately associate flags with languages, but instead with nationality (though that is obviously similar to language). I agree that the cropping of the flagpole is distracting, and the buoys as well. In terms of composition, I would prefer to see some more space in front of the boat, and maybe have the boat be a bit lower in the frame as well, to more coincide with the rule of thirds as well as foregoing a centered subject. The midday lighting is very harsh and leads to extreme shadow and light. Going by your photo details, I would definitely suggest you lower your ISO for shots like this. Another tip in regards to settings would be to resist from stopping down your aperture completely. At a certain  point, stopping down will actually start to decrease the sharpness of photos due to diffraction. Your colors are well saturated, and the flags have great pop to them.<br/>As I said, I really like where you were going with business as a language, I just think it was a bit hard for viewers to grasp that intent without reading your comments.<br/>-Derek<br/></td>', 'Hello, Im from the Critique Club; just stopping by drop you a comment!As a relatively new member, I hope youre enjoying DPC, and Im glad to see youve already entered a couple challenges.At first glance, this image wouldnt strike me as a language entry, for a couple reasons. After reading your comments, I understand where you were going with this and I like the idea of depicting commerce as a language, truly the only real international language, in fact. Unfortunately, viewers cannot see your comments during voting, so the fact that this is a fruit vendor would have been lost on me, as I didnt realize that without the help of your details. I also dont immediately associate flags with languages, but instead with nationality (though that is obviously similar to language). I agree that the cropping of the flagpole is distracting, and the buoys as well. In terms of composition, I would prefer to see some more space in front of the boat, and maybe have the boat be a bit lower in the frame as well, to more coincide with the rule of thirds as well as foregoing a centered subject. The midday lighting is very harsh and leads to extreme shadow and light. Going by your photo details, I would definitely suggest you lower your ISO for shots like this. Another tip in regards to settings would be to resist from stopping down your aperture completely. At a certain  point, stopping down will actually start to decrease the sharpness of photos due to diffraction. Your colors are well saturated, and the flags have great pop to them.As I said, I really like where you were going with business as a language, I just think it was a bit hard for viewers to grasp that intent without reading your comments.-Derek', '2009-04-06 02:02:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(5421052, 776091, 104944, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Mephisto:</b></div><hr/><i> taken with an h3d? for real? *envy*</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Yes, but it was taken with a 300mm (not 80mm as it says)<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Mephisto:</b></div><hr/><i> i actually quite like this shot, thought the concept of "body language" was quite obvious for this challenge and am a little confused this ended up so low down in the results.<br/>imo the composition works well, with the focus on the man who is about to pass by these teenagers, who on the other side are probably gonna challenge the man by not giving him enough space to pass. it makes me wonder what''s gonna happen next anyways. good work! </i></td></tr></table> <br/>The lighting should have stronger on the man in focus, you can see his eyes in full res and he really stands out - but it doesn''t have the same effect when resized.<br/><img alt="'' . substr(''//foto.perjorgen.com/dpchallenge/body_language_in_public_detail.jpg'', strrpos(''//foto.perjorgen.com/dpchallenge/body_language_in_public_detail.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//foto.perjorgen.com/dpchallenge/body_language_in_public_detail.jpg"/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-04-06 09:04:56.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by Mephisto: taken with an h3d? for real? *envy* Yes, but it was taken with a 300mm (not 80mm as it says) Originally posted by Mephisto: i actually quite like this shot, thought the concept of "body language" was quite obvious for this challenge and am a little confused this ended up so low down in the results.imo the composition works well, with the focus on the man who is about to pass by these teenagers, who on the other side are probably gonna challenge the man by not giving him enough space to pass. it makes me wonder what''s gonna happen next anyways. good work!  The lighting should have stronger on the man in focus, you can see his eyes in full res and he really stands out - but it doesn''t have the same effect when resized.', '2009-04-06 05:14:55.000000', '2009-04-06 10:04:56.000000', 0),
(5421112, 774984, 15407, '<td valign="top">Well I guess if the ''kaiser chief'' gets to Ireland he''ll need to be careful where he fishes! "I am not sure the silver thing was needed" Hard to imagine that he couldn''t tell it was an Irish Gold ring!... makes me wonder if he has a glass eye? I didn''t get a chance to vote this challenge yet I would have given it a score of 9!<br/>TK</td>', 'Well I guess if the ''kaiser chief'' gets to Ireland he''ll need to be careful where he fishes! "I am not sure the silver thing was needed" Hard to imagine that he couldn''t tell it was an Irish Gold ring!... makes me wonder if he has a glass eye? I didn''t get a chance to vote this challenge yet I would have given it a score of 9!TK', '2009-04-06 07:38:33.000000', NULL, 0),
